</sidebyside>

    <p>
      Select <em>one</em> problem from the list below.
      Write a complete, well-organized solution and justify
      each step of your work.
    </p>

    <p role="separator"></p>

  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="1prob-1">
    <title>Problem 1</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        One of the major problems in applied mathematics is
        finding an interpolation polynomial, that is, a
        polynomial of degree <m>n</m> whose graph passes
        through <m>n+1</m> given points.
      </p>

      <p>
        Find an equation of a cubic polynomial passing
        through the points
      </p>

      <me>
      (0,1),\quad (1,-2),\quad (2,0),\quad (3,2).
      </me>
    </statement>

    <workspace/>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="1prob-2">
    <title>Problem 2</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether the following set is linearly
        independent. Does it span
        <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m>? Explain why or why not.
      </p>

      <me>
      \left\{
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4\\5\\2\\6
      \end{bmatrix},
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2\\3\\1\\3
      \end{bmatrix},
      \begin{bmatrix}
      6\\3\\3\\8
      \end{bmatrix},
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4\\1\\5\\6
      \end{bmatrix}
      \right\}.
      </me>
    </statement>

    <workspace/>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="1prob-3">
    <title>Problem 3</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Find all values of <m>h</m> that make
      </p>

      <me>
      \mathbf b=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      9\\12\\h
      \end{bmatrix}
      </me>

      <p>
        a linear combination of
      </p>

      <me>
      \mathbf a_1=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1\\4\\4
      \end{bmatrix},
      \qquad
      \mathbf a_2=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      6\\8\\5
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </statement>

    <workspace/>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="1prob-4">
    <title>Problem 4</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether the system is consistent.
        If it is consistent, use Gaussian elimination
        to find:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>A parametric vector form of the solution set.</li>
        <li>One particular solution.</li>
      </ol>

      <me>
      \left\{
      \begin{aligned}
      2x_1+x_2+3x_3+4x_4 &amp; = 1\\
      x_1+x_2+x_3+x_4 &amp; = 2\\
      4x_1+2x_2+3x_3-x_4 &amp; = 1
      \end{aligned}
      \right.
      </me>
    </statement>

    <workspace/>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="prob-5">
    <title>Problem 5</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the solution sets for all possible values of
        the parameters <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> in
        the system
      </p>

      <me>
      \left\{
      \begin{aligned}
      ax+by &amp; = c\\
      a^2x+b^2y &amp; = c^2
      \end{aligned}
      \right.
      </me>
    </statement>

    <workspace/>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>